2026/02/21 08:46:54 INFO dspy.teleprompt.gepa.gepa: Iteration 7: Proposed new text for self: text
TASK
You are given a transcript of a clinical bedside nursing handover (outgoing→incoming nurse; may include patient/carer). Your job is to output which handover categories are EXPLICITLY addressed in the transcript.

OUTPUT (STRICT)
- Return ONLY a single JSON object: {"labels":[...]}
- "labels" must be a JSON array of strings.
- Each string MUST be exactly one of the category keys listed below.
- No extra keys, no commentary, no markdown outside this JSON, no trailing text.

CATEGORIES (EXACT KEYS ONLY)
introduction_clinicians, introduction_patients, invitation, id_check,
sit_diagnosis, sit_events, sit_status,
bg_history, bg_fall_risk, bg_pi_risk, bg_allergies, bg_acp,
assess_obs, assess_pain, assess_devices, assess_monitoring, assess_nutrition,
assess_fluid_balance, assess_infusions, assess_medications, assess_pathology,
assess_mobility, assess_skin_integrity,
rec_discharge_plan, rec_actions, rec_plan, rec_patient_goals

CORE DECISION PRINCIPLE (MOST IMPORTANT)
Only label a category when the transcript has a clear, direct, specific statement that matches that category.
- Do NOT infer from context (e.g., “elderly” ≠ falls risk; “awaiting CT” ≠ event).
- Do NOT over-label. If unsure, omit.
- Many transcripts will have only a few labels.

WORKFLOW (REQUIRED)
1) Read the whole transcript once.
2) Second pass: for EACH category, find an explicit supporting phrase.
3) Add the label ONLY if you can point to an explicit phrase that satisfies the rules below.
4) Final pass: remove any label that relied on assumption, implication, or “sounds like”.

CRITICAL EDGE-CASE RULES (BASED ON COMMON ERRORS)

A) INTRODUCTIONS & INVITATION
- introduction_patients:
  Label ONLY if a clinician explicitly introduces themself to the patient/carer OR introduces another clinician to the patient (e.g., “I’m Sam, I’ll be looking after you today”; “This is Priya, she’ll be your nurse”).
  Do NOT label if the patient is merely greeted/addressed by name, or if staff speak to each other only.
- introduction_clinicians:
  Label ONLY if clinicians are introduced to each other as handover participants (e.g., “Hi Priya, I’m Tom handing over to you”; “This is Sam taking over from me”).
  Do NOT label if only one clinician name appears without explicit introduction.
- invitation:
  Label ONLY if patient/carer is asked for permission to do bedside handover OR invited to participate/ask questions (e.g., “Is it okay if we do handover here?” “Jump in anytime.”).

B) IDENTIFICATION CHECK (VERY STRICT; COMMONLY OVER-LABELLED)
- id_check:
  Label ONLY if ALL THREE are explicitly checked/verified:
  (1) name + (2) date of birth + (3) an ID number/URN/MRN is read/verified.
  If any piece is missing, DO NOT label id_check.
  “Check wristband” without reading an ID number ≠ id_check.
  Name + DOB only ≠ id_check.

C) SITUATION
- sit_diagnosis:
  Label if primary reason for admission/presenting problem/diagnosis is explicitly stated (e.g., “admitted with aortic stenosis”, “came in with severe allergy symptoms”, “sepsis”).
- sit_events:
  Label ONLY for significant events/complications that occurred (e.g., fall, deterioration, adverse reaction, procedure complication).
  Do NOT count “tests arranged/pending” or “procedure booked tomorrow” as an event by itself.
- sit_status:
  Label for explicit current state / what they are awaiting / what is happening now (e.g., “stable”, “awaiting review”, “booked for TAVI at 8am tomorrow”, “cultures pending”, “fasting from midnight”, “team has reviewed”).
  Note: “stable” and “no acute issues” are sit_status (and may also support assess_obs only if framed as obs/vitals stable—see below).

D) BACKGROUND / ALERTS
- bg_history:
  Label for explicit comorbidities or relevant past/social history (e.g., HTN, CKD, asthma, mild cognitive impairment, bereavement, language needs).
  Do NOT label if history is not actually stated.
- bg_allergies:
  Label for any explicit allergy statement including NKDA, “no known allergies”, “allergies not documented yet”, “need to confirm”.
  Also label when the presenting problem is explicitly “allergy symptoms” AND/OR asthma/allergy is explicitly discussed as history; BUT do not invent an “allergy list” unless stated.
- bg_acp:
  Label ONLY if resus status/goals of care/DNR is explicitly mentioned.
  Do NOT label merely because a substitute decision-maker is mentioned.
- bg_fall_risk / bg_pi_risk:
  Label ONLY if explicitly stated as falls risk / pressure injury risk / Braden score / precautions.
  Do NOT infer from needing assistance, age, or limited mobility.

E) ASSESSMENT (CURRENT CLINICAL DETAILS) — KEY SEPARATIONS
- assess_obs:
  Label if vital signs/observations are stated OR explicitly described as stable/being done (e.g., “vitals WNL”, “obs stable”, “continue routine obs”, Q-ADDS).
  If only “stable” is said with no reference to vitals/observations, treat primarily as sit_status; include assess_obs only when it clearly refers to vitals/observations.
- assess_monitoring (COMMONLY OVER-LABELLED):
  Label ONLY for specific enhanced monitoring named (telemetry, continuous ECG, neuro checks, hourly BGLs as monitoring, alarms, continuous SpO2, etc.).
  Do NOT label for “routine obs”, “keep a close eye”, “monitor temperature/resp status” unless a specific monitoring modality/frequency is explicitly named.
  Important: Telemetry counts ONLY if explicitly stated (“on telemetry”).
- assess_pain:
  Label ONLY if pain is actively assessed/discussed now (e.g., “Any pain?”, “no chest pain overnight” counts).
  Do NOT label if pain is only part of historical presenting complaint with no current assessment.
- assess_pathology:
  Label for labs/bloods/cultures/imaging mentioned as taken, pending, results, or to be obtained (e.g., “blood cultures taken/pending”, “CT arranged”).
- assess_devices (COMMONLY MISSED):
  Label if any device/line is explicitly mentioned, INCLUDING explicit absence (“no lines or drains”), OR “IV cannula”, PIVC, IDC/catheter, drains, CVC, etc.
  Do NOT label just because IV meds/fluids are mentioned without a line/device stated.
- assess_infusions:
  Label only if an infusion/continuous IV therapy is explicitly running (often with a rate or “IV fluids running”).
  IV antibiotics given as intermittent doses ≠ assess_infusions (that is assess_medications).
- assess_medications:
  Label when specific meds/therapies are named or clearly referenced as being given/changed (e.g., antibiotics, paracetamol, perindopril, frusemide, aspirin).
- assess_nutrition:
  Label for diet status (NBM/fasting), diet type (low sodium), food chart/feeding/nutrition restrictions.
  “Fasting from midnight” counts.
- assess_fluid_balance:
  Label ONLY for explicit I&O/urine output/fluid restriction/daily weights/balance.
  Do NOT label for IV fluids alone.
- assess_mobility (COMMONLY MISSED):
  Label for explicit mobility/assistance level (ambulant, self-caring, mobilising with assistance/independently, needs help with ADLs).
- assess_skin_integrity:
  Label for wounds/dressings/pressure areas/turning/skin issues explicitly mentioned.

F) RECOMMENDATIONS / PLAN — DIFFERENTIATE rec_plan vs rec_actions
- rec_plan:
  Label for ongoing plan/next steps (follow up referrals, continue obs, prep for theatre, chase results, arrange interpreter, dietitian review, pastoral care referral).
- rec_actions (USE VERY SPARINGLY; COMMONLY OVER-LABELLED):
  Label ONLY if an urgent/time-critical action is explicitly framed as urgent/STAT/critical escalation (e.g., “call MET now”, “must notify doctor immediately”, “stat bloods”).
  Do NOT label for routine continuation or standard next steps (“continue obs”, “follow up today”, “keep a close eye”, “let me know if changes”).
- rec_discharge_plan:
  Label ONLY if discharge/transfer/placement planning is explicitly discussed.
- rec_patient_goals (COMMONLY MISSED):
  Label ONLY when patient/carer is explicitly asked about needs/goals/preferences (e.g., “Is there anything you need right now?” “What do you prefer?”).
  Do NOT label for reassurance alone.

FINAL OUTPUT VALIDATION
- Ensure every label is backed by an explicit phrase.
- Remove any label that depends on inference.
- Output exactly: {"labels":["key1","key2",...]} with double quotes.
