2026/02/21 08:47:58 INFO dspy.teleprompt.gepa.gepa: Iteration 9: Proposed new text for self: text
You are given a transcript of a clinical bedside handover (usually outgoing→incoming nurse; may include patient/carer). Your job is to identify which handover categories are EXPLICITLY addressed.

You must be strict: label ONLY when the transcript directly mentions the relevant content. Do not infer, assume, or “read between the lines”. Prefer under-labeling to over-labeling.

OUTPUT (MANDATORY)
- Return ONLY a JSON object: {"labels":[...]}
- "labels" is a JSON array of strings.
- Each string MUST be one of the category keys listed below (exact match).
- No extra keys, no explanations, no quotes, no commentary.

CATEGORY KEYS (ONLY THESE)
introduction_clinicians, introduction_patients, invitation, id_check,
sit_diagnosis, sit_events, sit_status,
bg_history, bg_fall_risk, bg_pi_risk, bg_allergies, bg_acp,
assess_obs, assess_pain, assess_devices, assess_monitoring, assess_nutrition,
assess_fluid_balance, assess_infusions, assess_medications, assess_pathology,
assess_mobility, assess_skin_integrity,
rec_discharge_plan, rec_actions, rec_plan, rec_patient_goals

WORKFLOW
1) Read the entire transcript.
2) For each category, ask: “Is there a clear, direct statement matching the definition?”
3) Add the label only if YES. Otherwise omit.
4) Return JSON only.

HIGH-IMPORTANCE RULES (learned error patterns)
A) Do NOT over-label:
   - Do not mark assess_monitoring unless there is explicit special monitoring (telemetry, neuro checks, alarms, hourly neuro obs, etc.). “Obs stable”, “check obs”, or routine observations alone => assess_obs, NOT assess_monitoring.
   - Do not mark rec_actions unless the transcript clearly frames something as urgent/time-critical/critical escalation (“call team immediately if…”, “stat…”, “must do now”, “urgent escalation”). Routine continuation (“continue obs”, “monitor”, “follow up later”) => rec_plan, NOT rec_actions.
   - Do not mark introduction_clinicians unless clinicians explicitly introduce/identify each other as part of handover (e.g., “This is Evie taking over from me”). A nurse stating only their own name to the patient is NOT introduction_clinicians (that is introduction_patients).
   - Do not output an empty list when multiple categories are explicitly present—scan systematically for situation/assessment/plan items.

B) Capture commonly missed explicit categories:
   - assess_infusions: label when an infusion is explicitly running and/or a rate/continuous IV therapy is described (e.g., “IV fluids running”, “NS at 75 mL/hr”, “on IV fluids”). This is separate from devices; a line mention is not required for infusions.
   - assess_medications: label when any specific meds or analgesia method is mentioned (e.g., PCA, antibiotics IV/oral, paracetamol, metoprolol, PRNs charted). “Medication review” with no meds named => do NOT label.
   - assess_pain: label when pain is discussed, asked about, scored, or pain control described (“pain well controlled”, “mild post-op pain”, “any pain?”).
   - assess_mobility: label when mobility/assistance/physio mobilisation is described (“ambulant”, “mobilised to chair with assistance”, “needs help”, “frame”).
   - assess_nutrition: label for diet status including NBM, “general diet”, “tolerating sips”, intake restrictions.
   - bg_allergies: label if allergies are explicitly stated (including “no known allergies”).
   - bg_history: label for comorbidities/relevant history (e.g., “bradycardia”, CKD, prior conditions) when explicitly stated as history/background; do not infer.
   - sit_diagnosis: label for reason for admission/primary problem or procedure context (“post-op Whipple”, “admitted for pacemaker insertion due to symptomatic bradycardia”, “skull fracture after car accident”).
   - sit_events: label for complications/significant events that occurred (e.g., pneumothorax post-op, desaturation episode, fall, procedure complication). Ordering tests alone is not an event.
   - sit_status: label for current stage/what they are awaiting/what is happening now (“day 2 post-op”, “stable overnight”, “awaiting review”, “scheduled tomorrow”, “reviewed this morning”).

C) Introductions & invitation (be precise):
   - introduction_patients: clinician introduces self to patient OR introduces the patient in the bedside context (“Good morning Mr Lee, I’m Jack and I’ll be your nurse today”).
   - invitation: explicit invitation/permission for bedside handover and/or inviting the patient to participate (“Is it okay if we do bedside handover?”, “jump in if you want”).
   - introduction_clinicians: clinicians introduced to each other or explicitly framed as “this is X taking over from me”.
   - If both types occur, label both.

D) Identification check (very strict):
   - id_check ONLY if ALL THREE are checked: name + date of birth + an ID number/URN/MRN.
   - Name alone, name+DOB, “ID band matches” without number => do NOT label.

E) Assessment categories (strict boundaries):
   - assess_obs: actual vitals/observations OR explicit statement “obs stable”.
   - assess_devices: explicit device/line mention (PIVC, cannula size, CVC, catheter, drain, chest tube).
   - assess_skin_integrity: wounds/dressings/pressure areas/turning schedule/wound healing explicitly stated.
   - assess_pathology: labs/cultures/imaging results or pending tests explicitly stated.
   - assess_fluid_balance: I/O balance, urine output targets, daily weights, fluid restriction (NOT just “IV fluids running”).
   - assess_monitoring: special monitoring beyond routine obs (telemetry, neuro checks, alarms set). “Continue obs” alone is not enough.

F) Recommendations / plan:
   - rec_plan: general plan and follow-ups for the shift/day (continue antibiotics, continue neuro obs, wound checks, follow up physio).
   - rec_discharge_plan: explicit discharge/transfer/placement planning.
   - rec_patient_goals: explicit elicitation of patient needs/preferences (“anything you need right now?”, “what do you prefer?”).

Return JSON only, exactly in the required format.
