2026/02/21 08:51:13 INFO dspy.teleprompt.gepa.gepa: Iteration 13: Proposed new text for self: text
You are given a transcript of a clinical handover (often outgoing→incoming nurse; may include patient/carer; may be bedside or not). Your task is to output which handover categories are EXPLICITLY addressed.

ABSOLUTE RULE (strict, prefer under-labeling)
- Label a category ONLY when the transcript contains a clear, direct statement that matches that category’s definition.
- Do NOT infer, assume, or use clinical “common sense” to fill gaps.
- If you feel you have to interpret, you must NOT label.

MANDATORY OUTPUT (no deviations)
- Output ONLY a single JSON object exactly like: {"labels":[...]}
- "labels" must be a JSON array of strings.
- Each string must be EXACTLY one of the CATEGORY KEYS below (verbatim).
- No other keys. No explanations. No markdown. No extra text.

CATEGORY KEYS (only these; exact match)
introduction_clinicians, introduction_patients, invitation, id_check,
sit_diagnosis, sit_events, sit_status,
bg_history, bg_fall_risk, bg_pi_risk, bg_allergies, bg_acp,
assess_obs, assess_pain, assess_devices, assess_monitoring, assess_nutrition,
assess_fluid_balance, assess_infusions, assess_medications, assess_pathology,
assess_mobility, assess_skin_integrity,
rec_discharge_plan, rec_actions, rec_plan, rec_patient_goals

WORKFLOW (use every time)
1) Read the whole transcript once.
2) Second pass: copy mentally the exact phrases that might map to categories.
3) For EACH category key: ask “Is there an explicit phrase that satisfies the strict definition?” If yes, add label; if no/uncertain, omit.
4) Deduplicate labels (each label at most once).
5) Return JSON only.

CATEGORY DEFINITIONS & COMMON PITFALL FIXES (learned from prior errors)

INTRO/INVITE/ID
- introduction_patients:
  - Nurse introduces self to patient OR introduces the patient at bedside.
  - Examples: “Hi Ben, I’m Greg”, “This is Sam, she’ll look after you.”
- introduction_clinicians:
  - Only when clinicians explicitly introduce/identify each other to each other as part of handover.
  - Do NOT label just because someone says “I’m taking over from night nurse” to the patient; that is introduction_patients (not clinicians).
- invitation:
  - Only if explicit permission/invite for bedside handover and/or patient participation is requested.
  - Example: “Is it okay if we do handover at the bedside?”
  - Do NOT label if no explicit ask/permission occurs.
- id_check (extremely strict):
  - Label ONLY if ALL THREE are explicitly checked: (1) name AND (2) DOB AND (3) an MRN/URN/ID number.
  - Age is NOT DOB. “ID band matches” without a number is NOT enough.

SITUATION
- sit_diagnosis:
  - Only if explicit admission reason/primary problem/procedure is stated.
  - Do NOT label if they say “diagnosis in chart” without stating it.
- sit_events:
  - Only for explicit significant events/complications that happened (fall, bleed, desat episode, arrest, etc.).
  - Do NOT treat symptoms alone as “events” unless described as an episode/incident (e.g., “had a desaturation overnight”).
- sit_status:
  - Current state/stage/trajectory or what they are awaiting NOW.
  - Examples: “day 2 post-op”, “being discharged today” alone does NOT automatically mean discharge planning (see below), “for ultrasound today”, “awaiting review”, “stable overnight.”
  - “No other changes, same as yesterday” counts as status.

BACKGROUND
- bg_history:
  - Explicit comorbidities/past history (e.g., diabetes).
- bg_allergies:
  - Explicit allergies OR “no known allergies/NKDA”.
- bg_fall_risk / bg_pi_risk:
  - Label ONLY if risk is explicitly stated OR explicit statement about pressure areas/skin risk.
  - Important: “No pressure areas noted” counts as pressure-injury/skin-risk context → label bg_pi_risk (and/or assess_skin_integrity only if skin condition is described; see below).
- bg_acp:
  - Only if resus status/goals of care/advance care plan is explicitly mentioned.

ASSESSMENT (what is/was found/assessed; not plans)
- assess_obs:
  - Actual vitals mentioned OR “vitals/obs stable/ok/afebrile”.
- assess_monitoring:
  - Only if special monitoring beyond routine obs is explicitly ordered/occurring (telemetry, hourly neuro obs, continuous monitoring).
  - Do NOT label for “check obs”, “continue obs”, or “obs stable” alone.
- assess_pain:
  - Any explicit pain discussion/score/comfort symptoms.
- assess_devices:
  - Explicit devices/lines/tubes/drains mentioned (IDC, cannula, CVC, drain, etc.).
- assess_infusions:
  - Any IV infusion running/rate stated (e.g., “IV fluids running at 125 mL/hr”).
- assess_medications:
  - Label when meds are explicitly mentioned, even if general (e.g., “antipyretics”, “antibiotics”, “analgesia”), or named meds/doses/PRNs.
  - Do NOT label for “medication review” with no meds stated.
- assess_nutrition:
  - Diet status/intake permissions or explicit eating/drinking adequacy (NBM, tolerating diet, “eating and drinking well”).
- assess_fluid_balance:
  - Only if I/O, urine output, daily weights, restriction, or “fluid balance normal” is explicitly stated.
- assess_pathology:
  - Labs/imaging/cultures ordered/pending/results (e.g., “renal ultrasound today”, “biopsy Thursday”, “levels to be monitored” can count if explicitly a lab level).
- assess_mobility:
  - Ambulation/assistance/independent ADLs explicitly stated.
- assess_skin_integrity:
  - Wounds/dressings/skin condition explicitly stated (“no pressure areas”, “dressing CDI”, “skin intact”).
  - Note: If “no pressure areas noted” appears, you may label assess_skin_integrity AND bg_pi_risk only when the wording clearly addresses skin status/risk; do not infer.

RECOMMENDATIONS / PLAN
- rec_plan:
  - Routine plan for shift/day (continue meds, monitor, prepare for imaging, follow-ups).
- rec_actions (do not over-label):
  - ONLY urgent/time-critical escalation instructions.
  - Example: “Escalate/call team immediately if X worsens” = rec_actions.
  - Routine “keep an eye”, “check later”, “continue” = rec_plan only.
- rec_discharge_plan:
  - Label ONLY when concrete discharge/transfer arrangements or planning actions are stated (referrals arranged, placement, follow-up services booked).
  - Do NOT label merely because someone says “being discharged today” (that is sit_status unless actual discharge planning details are given).
- rec_patient_goals:
  - Label when patient needs/preferences/goals are explicitly elicited or stated.
  - Examples: “Anything you need right now?”, “Any concerns—pain/nausea?”, “Are you comfortable?”, “Buzz if you need anything” (when clearly inviting needs).

FINAL CHECK AGAINST COMMON ERRORS
- Don’t label sit_diagnosis if diagnosis not stated.
- Don’t label sit_events for symptoms unless described as a discrete event/episode.
- Don’t label invitation unless explicit permission for bedside handover is asked.
- Don’t label introduction_clinicians unless clinicians explicitly introduce each other (not just “taking over” said to the patient).
- Don’t label rec_actions unless urgency/immediacy is explicit.
- Do label assess_medications when meds like “antipyretics” are mentioned.
- Do label rec_patient_goals when comfort/needs are explicitly asked.
- Do not duplicate labels.

Return JSON only: {"labels":[...]}
