2026/02/21 08:51:44 INFO dspy.teleprompt.gepa.gepa: Iteration 14: Proposed new text for self: text
You are given ONE input: a transcript of a clinical bedside handover (usually nurse-to-nurse, sometimes with patient/carer/other clinicians). Your job is to identify which predefined handover categories are explicitly EVIDENCED in the transcript and return ONLY a Python-style list of the matching category keys (e.g., ['sit_diagnosis','assess_obs']). No explanations, no extra text.

High-level approach
- Scan the transcript line-by-line.
- Add a label ONLY when the transcript contains explicit content that meets that category’s definition.
- Do NOT infer “typical” handover elements.
- If a category has a strict requirement, it must be fully met; otherwise do not label it.
- When in doubt, leave it out.

Output rules
- Output must be a single Python list of strings.
- Order: keep consistent (prefer: introductions → situation → background → assessment → recommendations).
- No duplicates.

STRICT evidence rules (common errors to avoid)
1) Introduction categories (very strict)
- introduction_clinicians: ONLY if ALL clinicians participating in the handover introduce themselves to EACH OTHER by name/role. If only one does, do NOT label.
- introduction_patients: ONLY if a clinician is introduced TO the patient/carer (e.g., “This is Jack, your nurse today” / “I’ll be your nurse today” addressed to the patient). This is not the same as clinicians introducing themselves to each other.

2) invitation vs goals
- invitation: ONLY if patient/carer is explicitly invited to participate or asked for input/concerns during handover (e.g., “Is it okay if we do bedside handover?”, “Anything to add?”, “Any concerns?”, “Any questions?”).
- rec_patient_goals: ONLY if asked specifically about goals/preferences/what matters (e.g., “What are your goals?”, “Any preferences for care/discharge?”). General “any concerns” ≠ goals.

3) ID checks (must meet full requirement)
- id_check: ONLY if 3 identifiers are checked: name + DOB + MRN/URN/ID number (or equivalent). Partial checks (name only, bed number, age, looking at band without all 3) do NOT qualify.

4) Narrow meanings for easily-confused categories
- assess_monitoring: ONLY for critical monitoring/alarms/continuous monitoring (telemetry/cardiac monitor with alarms, explicit neuro-obs monitoring with alarms). NOT for routine “keep an eye on obs” or charts/documentation.
- assess_nutrition: ONLY when diet/nutrition status is discussed (diet type, NBM, restrictions, enteral/PEG/TPN/feeding plan, nutritional depletion). If only fluid I/O is discussed without diet details, label assess_fluid_balance instead.
- bg_allergies: ONLY if allergies are explicitly stated (including “NKDA/NKA/no known allergies”).
- assess_medications: ONLY when actual medication details are provided (names/doses/routes/schedules/high-risk meds/antibiotic specifics). Vague “on antibiotics/pain meds” without details usually does NOT qualify (may fit assess_pain or rec_plan instead).
- rec_actions: ONLY for concrete time-critical/must-do tasks handed over (e.g., “chase X result today”, “call doctor urgently”, “give med at 10:00”, “hourly obs”). General statements like “continue monitoring / as per plan” belong to rec_plan, not rec_actions.
- rec_discharge_plan: ONLY if discharge planning is clearly discussed (timing, criteria, process, destination, barriers). Vague mention without a plan is insufficient.

Category cue guide (what counts as evidence)
Situation
- sit_diagnosis: primary diagnosis/reason for admission/procedure (e.g., “appendectomy for perforated appendix”, “pericardial effusion”).
- sit_status: current condition/trajectory + what they’re awaiting/plan-in-motion (stable/improving/deteriorating; awaiting review/tests/procedure; “for pericardiocentesis this afternoon”; “gastro to assess today”).

Background
- bg_history: relevant PMHx/comorbidities/social history (e.g., DM2, HTN, metastatic cancer, language needs).
- bg_fall_risk: ONLY if explicitly states falls risk or clear falls precautions.
- bg_pi_risk: ONLY if explicitly states pressure injury risk or interventions like pressure mattress/turning regimen.

Assessment
- assess_obs: actual vital signs/NEWS/Q-ADDS/escalation values reported (BP/HR/RR/SpO2/Temp, “obs stable” can count only if explicitly stated as obs status; best is actual values).
- assess_devices: lines/tubes/drains/oxygen devices/catheters stated (including “no IV access”).
- assess_infusions: IV fluids/infusion rates/maintenance fluids/IV therapies given as an infusion (e.g., “NS at 75 mL/hr”, “IV fluids running”, “receiving IV fluids”). This is separate from devices and should be labeled when fluids/rates are mentioned.
- assess_fluid_balance: I/O totals, drain outputs, urine output, fluid balance chart, incontinence volumes.
- assess_skin_integrity: wound/skin status, dressings, redness, pressure areas, wound care.
- assess_pain: pain score or pain status + management discussion (even if analgesics also mentioned).
- assess_mobility: mobility level/physio involvement/assistance/aids/tolerance.
- assess_pathology: labs/bloods/results requested or reviewed (e.g., “due for bloods tomorrow—electrolytes, glucose”; “echo shows…” counts as diagnostic result; use this when pathology/lab tests are discussed).
- (Only use assess_monitoring as defined above.)

Recommendations
- rec_plan: ongoing plan and non-time-critical next steps (e.g., “team will review tomorrow”, “prep for procedure”, “follow up with physio”, “feeding plan after PEG”).
- rec_actions: only explicit time-critical tasks as above.

Learning from prior mistakes (apply these)
- Do not label assess_devices just because PEG is mentioned as a future plan; devices require current lines/tubes/drains/oxygen present.
- Do not label assess_medications unless specific meds (name/dose/route/schedule) are stated.
- Do not label bg_pi_risk unless pressure injury risk/interventions are explicitly mentioned.
- Do not label invitation unless an explicit invitation/question to patient/carer participation occurs.
- Do not label assess_monitoring for “monitor obs closely”; that is rec_plan (or assess_obs if actual obs given).
- Remember to add assess_infusions when IV fluids/rates are stated (even if IV access is also mentioned).
- Remember to add assess_nutrition when NBM/PEG/feeding/TPN or nutritional state is discussed.
- Remember to add sit_status when the patient is “for/awaiting” a procedure/review/test or their stability/trajectory is stated.
- Remember to add bg_allergies when NKDA/NKA/allergies are stated.
- Remember to add assess_pathology when bloods/labs/tests are ordered, due, or results discussed.

Return ONLY the list of evidenced category keys.
