2026/02/21 09:00:17 INFO dspy.teleprompt.gepa.gepa: Iteration 24: Proposed new text for self: text
You are given a transcript of a clinical bedside nursing handover (outgoing → incoming nurse; may include patient/carer). Your job is to output which handover categories are EXPLICITLY addressed in the transcript.

CORE PRINCIPLE (strict)
- Label ONLY when the transcript directly states the relevant information.
- Do NOT infer, assume, or “read between the lines”.
- When unsure, DO NOT label (prefer under-labeling to over-labeling).

MANDATORY OUTPUT FORMAT
- Return ONLY a JSON object: {"labels":[...]}
- "labels" must be a JSON array of strings.
- Each string MUST be exactly one of the category keys listed below.
- No extra keys. No explanations. No commentary.

CATEGORY KEYS (only these; exact match)
introduction_clinicians, introduction_patients, invitation, id_check,
sit_diagnosis, sit_events, sit_status,
bg_history, bg_fall_risk, bg_pi_risk, bg_allergies, bg_acp,
assess_obs, assess_pain, assess_devices, assess_monitoring, assess_nutrition,
assess_fluid_balance, assess_infusions, assess_medications, assess_pathology,
assess_mobility, assess_skin_integrity,
rec_discharge_plan, rec_actions, rec_plan, rec_patient_goals

STEP-BY-STEP WORKFLOW
1) Read the entire transcript end-to-end.
2) For each category key, ask: “Is there a clear, direct statement that matches the category definition?”
3) Add the label ONLY if yes; otherwise omit.
4) Return JSON only.

CATEGORY DEFINITIONS + COMMON PITFALLS (must follow)

A) Introductions & invitation (be precise)
- introduction_patients:
  - clinician introduces self to patient OR introduces patient at bedside as part of handover.
  - Examples: “Good morning Mr Lee, I’m Jack and I’ll be your nurse today”; “This is Mr X in bed 6.”
- introduction_clinicians:
  - ONLY if clinicians explicitly introduce/identify each other in the handover/takeover sense.
  - Example: “Hi Evie, this is Jack handing over to you / Evie taking over from me.”
  - NOT met by: a nurse saying only their own name to the patient.
- invitation:
  - ONLY if explicit permission/invitation for bedside handover or patient participation.
  - Examples: “Is it okay if we do bedside handover?”, “Jump in if you want”, “We’ll do bedside handover… if that’s okay.”
  - NOT met by: “we’re doing handover” without asking/inviting.

B) Identification check (very strict)
- id_check ONLY if ALL THREE are checked explicitly:
  1) name AND 2) date of birth AND 3) an ID number (MRN/URN/etc.).
- Name alone, name + DOB, age, bed number, “ID band matches” without number => NO id_check.

C) Situation (SIT)
- sit_diagnosis:
  - reason for admission / primary problem / procedure context.
  - Examples: “came in with severe headache”, “admitted for bowel management due to constipation”, “admitted with recurrent lung cancer”.
- sit_events:
  - complications or significant events that occurred (fall, desaturation episode, pneumothorax, etc.).
  - Tests ordered alone are NOT an “event” unless a result/event is stated.
- sit_status:
  - current stage/state/trajectory: “stable overnight”, “day 2 post-op”, “awaiting review”, “now admitted”, “comfort measures only”, “no routine obs”.
  - Note: statements like “no routine observations” / “vitals stable” / “settled” can count as sit_status if describing current status.

D) Background (BG)
- bg_history:
  - explicit past medical history/comorbidities/background (“COPD”, “smoking history”, “previous chemo/radiotherapy”).
  - Do NOT label for the current presenting complaint alone (that belongs in sit_diagnosis).
- bg_allergies:
  - allergies explicitly stated, including “no known allergies”.
- bg_fall_risk, bg_pi_risk, bg_acp:
  - label only if explicitly mentioned (e.g., “high falls risk”, “pressure injury risk”, “advanced care plan / NFR / goals-of-care document”).

E) Assessment (A) — strict boundaries
- assess_obs:
  - actual vitals/observations OR explicit statement “obs/vitals stable”.
  - Also label if they explicitly state “no routine observations” (it is still an observations/monitoring decision being stated).
- assess_monitoring:
  - ONLY special monitoring beyond routine obs (telemetry, hourly neuro obs, alarms, neuro checks).
  - “Obs stable”, “check obs”, “monitor” without special monitoring => NOT assess_monitoring.
- assess_pain:
  - any mention of pain presence/absence, score, or pain control (“pain 3/10”, “headache”, “pain well controlled”, “any pain?”).
- assess_medications:
  - ONLY when at least one medication/analgesia is explicitly named or clearly referenced as administered (e.g., “Endone”, “Panadol”, “antibiotics”, “PCA”).
  - If they only say “as charted” with NO medication name, do NOT label assess_medications.
- assess_mobility:
  - explicit mobility/assistance (“mobilising with assistance”, “ambulant”, “to chair”, “frame”).
- assess_nutrition:
  - diet/intake status (“reduced appetite”, “tolerating fluids”, “NBM”, “general diet”).
- assess_devices:
  - explicit devices/lines/drains/catheters/chest tubes, etc.
- assess_infusions:
  - infusion explicitly running and/or rate (IV fluids running, NS at 75 mL/hr).
  - Separate from devices; a cannula mention alone does not equal infusion.
- assess_fluid_balance:
  - I/O balance, urine output amounts/targets, daily weight, restriction (not just “on IV fluids”).
- assess_pathology:
  - explicit labs/imaging/culture results OR pending tests (“X-ray showed faecal loading”, “bloods pending”).
- assess_skin_integrity:
  - wounds/dressings/pressure areas/turning schedule.

F) Recommendations/Plan (R)
- rec_plan:
  - routine plan for shift/day (continue analgesia, continue obs, follow up teams, referrals, comfort measures).
  - Includes follow-up actions that are NOT framed as urgent.
- rec_actions:
  - ONLY urgent/time-critical escalation instructions (“call team immediately if…”, “stat…”, “must do now”, “urgent escalation”).
  - Routine “let the team know if anything changes” => rec_plan, NOT rec_actions.
- rec_discharge_plan:
  - ONLY explicit discharge/transfer planning (e.g., “discharge tomorrow”, “awaiting placement”, “transfer to rehab”).
  - Social work referral alone without discharge/placement statement => NOT rec_discharge_plan.
- rec_patient_goals:
  - explicit elicitation of patient needs/preferences/goals right now (“Anything you need right now?”, “as you prefer”).

QUALITY CHECKS BASED ON COMMON ERRORS (apply every time)
- Do NOT label bg_history when the transcript only describes the current presenting problem (that is sit_diagnosis).
- Do NOT label rec_discharge_plan for social work/home support referrals unless discharge/placement is explicitly discussed.
- Do NOT label introduction_clinicians unless the handover explicitly identifies the clinicians to each other as outgoing/incoming.
- Do NOT label id_check unless name + DOB + MRN/URN are all checked.
- Do NOT label assess_medications unless a medication/therapy is explicitly named (avoid labeling for “analgesia as charted” alone).
- Do label sit_diagnosis when the reason for admission/presenting complaint is directly stated.
- Do label bg_allergies when “no allergies” or specific allergies are stated.
- Do label invitation only when permission/invitation language is used.

Return JSON only in the required format.
