2026/02/21 09:06:24 INFO dspy.teleprompt.gepa.gepa: Iteration 31: Proposed new text for self: text
TASK
You are given a transcript of a clinical bedside handover (outgoing→incoming nurse; may include patient/carer/doctor). Output which handover categories are EXPLICITLY addressed in the transcript.

OUTPUT (STRICT)
- Return ONLY a single JSON object: {"labels":[...]}
- "labels" must be a JSON array of strings.
- Each string MUST be exactly one of the category keys listed below.
- No extra keys, no commentary, no trailing text.

CATEGORIES (EXACT KEYS ONLY)
introduction_clinicians, introduction_patients, invitation, id_check,
sit_diagnosis, sit_events, sit_status,
bg_history, bg_fall_risk, bg_pi_risk, bg_allergies, bg_acp,
assess_obs, assess_pain, assess_devices, assess_monitoring, assess_nutrition,
assess_fluid_balance, assess_infusions, assess_medications, assess_pathology,
assess_mobility, assess_skin_integrity,
rec_discharge_plan, rec_actions, rec_plan, rec_patient_goals

CORE DECISION PRINCIPLE (NON-NEGOTIABLE)
Only label a category when the transcript contains a CLEAR, DIRECT, SPECIFIC mention that matches the category definition.
- Do NOT infer (e.g., “limited mobility” ≠ falls risk unless “falls risk” is explicitly stated).
- When uncertain, OMIT.
- Many transcripts will have only a few labels.

WORKFLOW (MENTAL; OUTPUT JSON ONLY)
1) Scan for exact phrases that map to category definitions.
2) For each candidate label, ensure you can quote a supporting phrase.
3) Apply the “common traps” below—these are frequent sources of over-labeling.
4) Output JSON with unique labels (order doesn’t matter).

DEFINITIONS + HIGH-FREQUENCY ERROR TRAPS

A) INTRODUCTIONS & INVITATION
- introduction_patients:
  Label ONLY if a clinician introduces themself to the patient/carer OR introduces another clinician to the patient.
  Examples: “I’m Sarah and I’ll be your nurse today”; “This is Samantha who’ll be looking after you now.”
  NOT enough: addressing patient by name only (“Hi Vera”).
- introduction_clinicians:
  Label ONLY if clinicians explicitly introduce/identify themselves to EACH OTHER as handover participants.
  Trap: patient-facing intros (“I’m Samantha”) are NOT automatically clinician-to-clinician introductions.
- invitation:
  Label ONLY if patient/carer is explicitly invited/asked permission to participate/stay/contribute in handover.
  Examples: “Is it okay if we do bedside handover?” “Jump in anytime.”
  Trap: reassurance/help statements are NOT invitations.

B) IDENTIFICATION CHECK (VERY STRICT)
- id_check:
  Label ONLY if ALL THREE identifiers are explicitly checked:
  (1) name AND (2) date of birth AND (3) an ID number/URN/MRN is read/verified.
  Traps:
  - Name + DOB only → NO.
  - “ID band matches” without stating/verifying an ID number → NO.

C) SITUATION
- sit_diagnosis:
  Label when the presenting problem/primary diagnosis/reason for admission is explicitly stated.
  Examples: “presented with chest pain”; “metastatic pancreatic cancer”.
- sit_events:
  Label for significant events/complications/procedures that occurred.
  Example: “had an angiogram today”, “fell overnight”.
  Trap: tests ordered/pending are NOT events (use assess_pathology or rec_plan instead).
- sit_status:
  Label for explicit current status / what they are awaiting / current phase.
  Examples: “now in recovery”; “for D&C later today”; “awaiting review”; “on protocol”; “actively dying”.
  Trap: do NOT label sit_status just because ongoing care is discussed; it must be a clear “current status/awaiting/for X” statement.

D) BACKGROUND / ALERTS
- bg_history:
  Label ONLY for explicit past history/comorbidities (stroke, asthma, CKD, etc.) or relevant social history.
  Trap: the acute presenting problem belongs to sit_diagnosis, not bg_history.
- bg_allergies:
  Label for ANY explicit mention (“NKDA”, “no known allergies”, “need to confirm allergies”).
  Trap: don’t forget this—often missed.
- bg_acp:
  Label for resus status/goals-of-care/ARP/DNR explicitly mentioned.
  Trap: end-of-life context alone doesn’t count unless resus/escalation status is explicitly stated (e.g., “ARP in place”).
- bg_fall_risk / bg_pi_risk:
  Must explicitly state falls risk or pressure injury risk/score/precautions.
  Traps:
  - “limited mobility”, “needs assistance”, “elderly” ≠ falls risk unless “falls risk” is said.
  - Pressure injury/wounds ≠ “pressure injury risk” unless risk/score/mattress/turning framed as risk/precautions (e.g., “Waterlow 20”, “high risk”, “pressure-relieving mattress”, “repositioning q2h”).

E) ASSESSMENT (CURRENT CLINICAL DETAILS)
- assess_obs:
  Label if vitals/observations are stated OR explicitly described as stable or being done.
  Examples: “Vitals stable”; RR 8–10; “4-hourly obs”; “Q-ADDS”.
  Trap: Q-ADDS counts as assess_obs (not assess_monitoring).
- assess_monitoring:
  Label ONLY for specific enhanced monitoring modality named.
  Examples: “continuous monitoring”, “telemetry”, “continuous cardiac monitoring”, “neuro checks”.
  Trap: “monitor closely” without modality → NO.
- assess_pain:
  Label when current pain state/score or pain check is discussed.
  Examples: “pain score 2/10”; “pain free now”; “are you comfortable?”
  Trap: pain as historical presenting complaint only → NO.
- assess_pathology:
  Label for labs/bloods/cultures/imaging results OR pending/ordered/arranged.
- assess_devices:
  Label when a device/line is explicitly mentioned (IDC, catheter, cannula/PIVC, CVC, drain, etc.).
- assess_infusions:
  Label when IV fluids/infusion therapy is explicitly mentioned (running or explicitly “no fluids running”).
  Trap: IV meds alone ≠ infusion unless an infusion/fluid is stated.
- assess_medications:
  Label when meds/therapies are explicitly discussed (by name/class/route) or “no meds”.
  Examples: nitro sprays, morphine given, syringe driver contents.
  Trap: don’t miss medications just because they are part of describing an event.
- assess_nutrition:
  Label when diet/NBM/oral intake/refeeding is explicitly discussed.
  Example: “no oral intake since yesterday”.
- assess_fluid_balance:
  Requires explicit I/O or “fluid balance chart” or amounts/targets.
  Trap: IDC present or “urine output ceased” DOES count as output mention? Only label if explicitly fluid balance/I&O framing or measurable output described. If it only says “IDC in place draining clear urine” without I&O/balance language, do NOT label.
- assess_mobility:
  Label for explicit mobility/assist level (needs assistance with ADLs, not mobilised, supervised mobilisation).
- assess_skin_integrity:
  Label for explicit wounds/dressings/skin/pressure areas/turning/pressure care monitoring.
  Examples: stage 2 PI, dressing type, “pressure areas monitored”, “repositioning q2h”.

F) RECOMMENDATIONS / PLAN
- rec_plan:
  Label for ongoing plan/next steps/routine instructions/referrals/follow-up.
  Examples: “continue pressure care”, “keep reassessing pain”, “notify doctor if pain changes”, “continue regular checks”.
  Trap: “notify if…” is usually rec_plan unless explicitly immediate/STAT.
- rec_actions (USE VERY SPARINGLY):
  Label ONLY for urgent/time-critical escalation framed as immediate.
  Examples: “call doctor now”, “STAT”, “immediately”.
  Trap: routine “continue obs/monitoring/follow up” ≠ rec_actions.
- rec_discharge_plan:
  Label ONLY if discharge/transfer/placement planning is explicitly discussed as a discharge/placement plan.
  Trap: bedside logistics (“in bed 3”, “moved rooms”) ≠ discharge plan.
- rec_patient_goals:
  Label ONLY if patient is explicitly asked about needs/goals/preferences.
  Example: “Is there anything you need right now before we finish?”

FINAL CHECK (ANTI-OVERLABELING)
Before outputting each label, confirm there is an explicit supporting phrase. If you can’t point to a phrase, omit the label.
Return valid JSON: {"labels":["sit_diagnosis","assess_obs"]}.
